  "variation": {
    "seeded": true,
    "notes": "The inflow streaks, arcs, beam shudder, blast billows, debris, crater texture and shake vary per cast. The 0.85 s charge, the 0.1 s overload, the fire frame, the beam length and the 1.1 s recharge stay fixed: the charge is the dodge window and the recharge is the punish window, so both must be the same every time."
  },
  "director_notes": "An original all-out energy beam: the archetype trades a long charge and a helpless recharge for one huge hit, so both ends of the timeline are part of the design. Choices: (1) a 0.85 s charge where energy is dragged IN (sparks gather_px off the ground, a closing ring, arcs over the hand, cracks spreading under the feet) plus a 0.1 s overload flicker as the last dodge cue; (2) the release sells recoil on the caster's side: a white flash and one screen blink, a shockwave ring standing up across the line of fire (ring plane vertical), dust blasting out along the ground, and a skid scorch stretched back behind the caster; in a game the caster should also be knocked back a little; (3) the beam is huge (70-80 px, wider than a body) with three strands corkscrewing along it (spiral_count 3) so it reads as a 3D column from every view, and a red-crimson palette with a white core keeps it apart from fire (no flame texture) and from lightning (no jagged forks); (4) a full explosion at the target (flash, energy billows, shockwave, debris, crater over a red-hot burn, smoke column) and a 1.1 s recharge where the caster vents smoke and sputters. Sound: charge, crackle on the overload, crack, a low hum for the beam, boom plus rumble, a quiet sizzle while recharging."
}
